Additional packages installed from the Red Hat UBI repositories carry their own
upstream licenses (predominantly GPL, LGPL, MIT, and BSD variants). For the
authoritative, per-package list for a built image, run:

    rpm -qa --qf '%{NAME} %{VERSION} %{LICENSE}\n' | sort

HAProxy's GPL text includes an exception permitting linking with OpenSSL
specifically, which doesn't name AWS-LC. That exception isn't actually needed
here, though: haproxy-awslc is GPL-2.0-*or-later* (confirmed via its RPM
LICENSE metadata), so the combined work can be distributed under GPLv3 terms,
and the FSF recognizes Apache-2.0 (AWS-LC's license) as GPLv3-compatible.

Enabling the ACME agent means whoever runs the container is agreeing to Let's
Encrypt's Subscriber Agreement (https://letsencrypt.org/repository/) on
account registration, and to Porkbun's API Terms of Service if DNS-01 via
Porkbun is used -- usage-policy matters for the operator, not redistribution
terms for this repository.

